# Operator-only tooling. Kept out of the public repo under the publishing
# guide's rule against exposing local paths: build_index.py resolves the
# canonical source inside the private thesis corpus, and HANDOFF.md documents
# machine paths and internal decisions. Both are backed up in the private
# sovereign-infra repository.
scripts/build_index.py
HANDOFF.md

# Superseded build inputs and unapproved candidates are operator-only: they
# are frozen inputs and pre-approval drafts, not the published product. These
# are globs on purpose. The v8.0 rule was a literal filename, so v8.2 and the
# v8.3 candidate were not covered when the vintage advanced and were staged
# for the public repo by accident. A rule's scope has to travel with it.
data/qesis_v*_superseded.json
data/qesis_v*_CANDIDATE.json

# Release backups written by the staging scripts, e.g. qesis_v8.json.pre-v8.5.bak.
# Git history is the real backup; a stray copy of the index in the tree is a
# second artefact with the same name and no binding, which is how a reader ends
# up citing bytes nothing published.
data/*.bak

# Never publishable on any channel.
# INC-20260805-01. The rule was `.env` plus `.env.*`, which matches `.env` and
# `.env.local` but NOT `.env2` -- `.env.*` requires the dot. A file called
# `.env2` holding ENTSOE_API_KEY was therefore not ignored, was committed in
# 5dfd876, and reached a public repository. That is the second time this exact
# credential has escaped through a naming gap: INC-20260731-01 was
# "ENTSO-E API KEY.txt" slipping past `*.key`, which matches an extension and
# not a name. A prefix rule is used here so the next `.env` variant is covered
# before anyone invents it.
.env*
*.key
*.pem
credentials*.json
service-account*.json
# INC-20260731-01, G-03. The rules above did not cover a file called
# "ENTSO-E API KEY.txt": `*.key` matches an extension, not a name. A credential
# reached a synced folder with nothing standing in its way, and the only reason
# it never reached this repository is that nobody happened to copy it here.
_SCHEMA/
*API*KEY*

# Local noise
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
.vercel/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
node_modules/

# Large binaries do not belong in a product repo
*.pdf
*.ipynb

# Not part of the published product
docker-compose.yml
sovereign_infra_backup.sql
REPORT_EXECUTIVE.md

# Local MCP wiring: absolute machine paths, never published
.mcp.json

# Same rule, same reason, second directory. `.claude/settings.local.json` holds
# 257 permission rules carrying this machine's home path, and `.claude/` was
# neither tracked nor ignored, so one `git add -A` would have published all of
# it. `.claude/launch.json` is deliberately NOT ignored: it names relative
# commands and ports, describes the project rather than the host, and is worth
# sharing with anyone cloning this.
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/*.local.json
.env*
*.env
*secret*
*SECRET*
*credential*
sk_test_*
sk_live_*
github-mcp-server/

.env*
!.env.example

.vercel
.env*

# Raw source data held locally, never committed. Derived evidence under
# data/axes/ and data/fsqca/ carries SHA-256 hashes pinning these inputs.
data/electricity_yearly_*_EMBER.csv
data/ember_electricity_data_methodology.pdf

# Connection strings and local credential scratch. Never commit.
database_string.txt
*_connection_string.txt

# Graphify local codebase-navigation tool output (2026-08-15) - local aid only, never a source of truth
graphify-out/
graphify_src_DELETE_ME.tar.gz

# Staging area for files on their way out of the repository. Its whole purpose is
# to hold things that must NOT be committed, so it is ignored as a directory
# rather than by the names of whatever happens to be sitting in it today.
#
# Added 2026-08-15 after `_to_delete/graphify-out.zip` was committed at f14385b.
# The two rules above name a directory and a filename, and the zip matched
# neither: it is `graphify-out.zip`, not the `graphify-out/` directory. Ignoring
# by enumerated filename means the rule protects exactly the files you thought of
# and nothing else (L-124).
_to_delete/
